  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Terrestrial animals are generally either ureotelic or uricotelic, and not ammonotelic. Why? (3 marks)', 'Ammonia is very toxic and must be kept dilute; to excrete it an animal needs a large amount of water, so ammonotelism suits aquatic animals that live in plenty of water. Terrestrial animals have to conserve water, so they convert ammonia into less toxic forms that need less water to excrete: urea (made in the liver by the ornithine cycle), which is moderately soluble and less toxic - ureotelic animals such as mammals and adult amphibians; or uric acid, which is almost insoluble and can be excreted as a paste or pellets with very little water - uricotelic animals such as birds, reptiles and insects (useful also for animals developing in shelled eggs).', null, '[{"point": "Ammonia is very toxic and highly soluble - it needs large amounts of water to flush out (ammonotelic animals are mostly aquatic)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Land animals must conserve water, so they convert ammonia into less toxic urea (needs less water) or uric acid (almost insoluble, excreted as paste/ pellets)", "marks": 1.5}, {"point": "Examples: mammals and amphibians are ureotelic; birds, reptiles and insects are uricotelic", "marks": 0.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
