  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Explain the antibiotic resistance observed in bacteria in the light of Darwin''s theory of natural selection. (3 marks)', 'Darwin''s natural selection needs variation, selection and inheritance. (1) Variation: in a large population of bacteria, a few individuals already have genes for resistance, arising by chance mutation or gained from other bacteria by plasmids. (2) Selection: when an antibiotic is used, it kills the susceptible bacteria, while the resistant ones survive - the ''fittest'' in that environment. (3) Inheritance: the surviving resistant bacteria multiply rapidly and pass the resistance genes to their offspring (and to other bacteria), so over many generations the population becomes mostly resistant. Overuse, misuse and incomplete courses of antibiotics increase this selection pressure.', null, '[{"point": "Variation: in a bacterial population a few cells already carry resistance genes (by mutation or gene transfer)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Selection: the antibiotic kills susceptible bacteria; resistant ones survive (survival of the fittest)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Inheritance: survivors multiply and pass on resistance, so the population becomes resistant over generations; misuse/ incomplete courses speed this up", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge') and t.slug = 'bio-science-general-subject-knowledge'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-2'), 'standard', 3,
