  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'The probability of fruit set in a self-pollinated bisexual flower of a plant is far greater than in a dioecious plant. Explain. (3 marks)', 'In a bisexual flower that is self-pollinated, the anthers and stigma are in the same flower and often mature together, so pollen is transferred directly to the stigma (sometimes even before the flower opens, as in cleistogamous flowers). Pollination is assured and does not depend on outside agents, so the chance of fertilisation and fruit set is very high. A dioecious plant bears male and female flowers on separate plants, so pollen must be carried from one plant to another by wind, insects or other agents; this needs both sexes to grow nearby and to flower at the same time, and much pollen is lost on the way, so pollination and fruit set are far less certain.', null, '[{"point": "Bisexual self-pollinated flower: anthers and stigma in the same flower, so pollen reaches the stigma easily and reliably (often even before opening - cleistogamy)", "marks": 1.5}, {"point": "Dioecious plant: male and female flowers on separate plants - pollination depends on agents (wind, insects), distance and presence of both sexes; much pollen is wasted, so fruit set is less certain", "marks": 1.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge') and t.slug = 'bio-science-general-subject-knowledge'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-2'), 'standard', 3,
