  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'A transverse section of a tree trunk shows concentric dark and light coloured rings.
(a) What are these rings called? (1 mark)
(b) Why are the dark and light coloured rings of different thickness? (2 marks)', '(a) They are annual rings (growth rings). (b) The activity of the vascular cambium changes with the seasons. In spring (the favourable season) the cambium is very active and produces many xylem vessels with wide cavities and thin walls; this spring (early) wood is light in colour, less dense and forms a broad band. In autumn/ winter the cambium is less active and forms fewer, narrower elements with thick walls; this autumn (late) wood is darker, denser and forms a narrow band. One light band plus one dark band represents one year''s growth, which is why the rings differ in colour and thickness and can be used to estimate the age of the tree.', null, '[{"point": "(a) Annual (growth) rings", "marks": 1}, {"point": "(b) Spring/ early wood: cambium very active in spring/ favourable season - many wide, thin-walled vessels - light, broad band", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Autumn/ late wood: cambium less active - fewer, narrow, thick-walled elements - dark, narrow band; one light + one dark ring = one year''s growth", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
